# Inline citations must link

Whenever body copy names a source inline (`per the Daily Times`, `Knox News reported`, `according to Business Insider`, `cited by Tennessee Firefly`, `404 Media reviewed`, etc.), **the outlet or document name must be a direct `<a href="..." target="_blank" rel="noopener">` link** to that primary URL in the same sentence—not bare text with the link only in a Sources footer.

## Do

- Link the first clear attribution in the paragraph to the same URL used in that panel’s Sources list
- Prefer primary reporting / official records already cited on the page
- Keep a Sources footer as a bibliography; it does not replace the inline link

## Do not

- Ship “per the *Outlet*” / “*Outlet* reported” with an unlinked outlet name
- Invent a secondary URL just to satisfy the rule—if you cannot cite a real URL, drop or rephrase the named attribution
- Link every repeated “told *Outlet*” in the same paragraph after the first linked mention (first mention is enough)

## Related

- Still sync `sources.html` per `keep-sources-synced.mdc`
- Still sync downloadables when a story belongs in a dossier per `sync-site-downloadables-and-sources.mdc`
